+A visit's scientific fields are observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer these relationships from field names, so it does not combine edits field by field.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. A retry with the same idempotency key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result. Reuse of a key with a different payload is rejected and leaves the original submission unchanged.
+
+**Submission envelope.** Each submission carries:
+
+- visit ID and plot reference;
+- idempotency key;
+- author ID;
+- base revision ID, the revision the worker's draft was started from (absent for a visit's first submission);
+- the complete visit as the worker submitted it.
+
+**Acceptance outcomes.** An accepted submission is either the visit's current revision, or a competing revision. A rejected submission returns the reason. A competing revision is an accepted outcome, not an error.
+
+**Current-state response.** Each retrieval returns the visit's current revision and whether unresolved review work exists for that visit (REC-005). The response distinguishes these two things: a usable current revision is always present once a visit has one, and unresolved work is reported alongside it, not in place of it.
+
+## Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+**Policy: whole-visit revisions.** Each submission names the revision it started from and carries the complete visit. The reasoning, the rejected field-aware merge alternative, and the conditions for revisiting the choice are recorded in [decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md).
+
+**Rules.**
+
+1. A visit has one current revision. Before any competing revision exists, a submission whose base is the current revision becomes the next current revision.
+2. A submission whose base is any other revision becomes a competing revision.
+3. While a visit has unresolved competing revisions, every new submission for that visit is stored as a competing revision. Nothing advances the current revision automatically.
+4. The current revision stays at the last revision that preceded the first unresolved competing revision until a resolution makes a different revision current.
+5. A resolution records the coordinator's identity, the revision it makes current, and the IDs of the submissions it draws on (REC-006). Original submissions are never altered or removed by a resolution.
+
+Rule 3 is the rule most likely to need revision after the Phase 4 edge audit, since it determines how long a visit stays unresolved under sustained concurrent editing.
+
+## Downstream Obligations
+
+**Tablet-sync** must:
+
+- record the base revision ID and the full visit for every local draft, and keep that draft until the server returns an acceptance outcome;
+- resend the same idempotency key and payload after a lost response, and accept the original acceptance result as the answer;
+- present a competing-revision acceptance as a saved edit awaiting coordinator review, not as a failure;
+- refresh the current-state response on reconnection so pending competing revisions are shown to the worker;
+- size local storage by the number of visits a tablet edits, since each draft holds a whole visit.
+
+**Review-export** must:
+
+- provide a comparison view across the revisions of one visit, showing the current revision and each competing revision as submitted, with author and receipt time for each;
+- use the unresolved-work flag from the current-state response to show review queues and to decide export eligibility (see Open Questions, item 1);
+- trace every exported row to the revision it came from and, for a resolved visit, to the resolution and the submissions it draws on;
+- size coordinator review load by visits with competing revisions, including edits to unrelated fields of the same visit.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent visit edit policy | Whole-visit revisions with competing revisions for review | Field-aware three-way merge with automatic combination of disjoint field changes | Keeps each submitted visit intact and never creates a combined state no worker submitted. Review volume is the accepted cost. See [decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md). |
+| Current revision while competing revisions are unresolved | Current revision holds at the last pre-conflict revision; new submissions also compete | Advance the current revision on any submission that names it as base; hide the visit from retrieval until resolved | Keeps the current state limited to revisions a coordinator has accepted, and keeps the visit retrievable so the export and tablets can show it. |
+| Duplicate detection | Client-generated idempotency key per submission, identical payload returns the original result | Server-assigned submission IDs with no retry deduplication | Retries after a dropped connection are common on offline tablets; deduplication prevents one worker's edit from becoming two competing revisions. |
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+### Open
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+1. **Export of a visit with unresolved competing revisions.** Should the visit be omitted from the partner's CSV, exported at its current revision with a flag, or should the export wait until it is resolved? The partner receives this CSV weekly, and the tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* applies to the export as well as to submission.
+2. **Form of a resolution.** May a coordinator resolve by selecting an existing revision only, or may the coordinator compose a new revision from parts of several submissions? Composition gives more control and produces a state no worker submitted; selection keeps every resolved state traceable to one submission.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Deferred to the Phase 4 edge audit
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+3. A submission that arrives while a resolution is being recorded: does the resolution cover only the competing revisions it names, or every competing revision present when it is recorded? Rule 3 depends on this answer.
 
